"""
part2c_granger_lp.py  --  Panel LP Granger causality between shock series
==========================================================================
Examines whether the establishment-closing shock (δ) and the separation
shock (LD) are causally ordered or reflect independent structural disturbances.

Economic motivation
-------------------
The model requires both δ (product-line destruction) and s (match separation)
as primitive structural shocks.  δ might instead be largely endogenous —
rising as a lagged response when s has been high and firms have been losing
workers.  If so, it would be redundant to include both shocks, and the
model's prediction of a distinct δ transmission mechanism would be an
artifact of the specification.

We test this using a panel LP Granger causality design.  For each pair of
series (X, Y) we ask: do lags of X help predict Y above and beyond Y's own
lags, conditional on industry and time fixed effects?  The key questions are:

  (1) Does LD Granger-cause δ?
      If yes: closings are partly an endogenous response to prior separations.
      If no: δ is not forecastable from the history of s — consistent with
             it being driven by an independent structural disturbance.

  (2) Does δ Granger-cause LD?
      If yes and (1) is rejected: δ is the primitive shock, and separations
             at surviving firms follow as an endogenous consequence — exactly
             the mechanism the model has in mind for endogenous exit.
      If both directions are significant: shocks are jointly determined,
             and the structural interpretation requires a richer model.

Specification
-------------
For each pair (Y, X) and each horizon h = 0, ..., H we estimate:

    Y_{j,t+h} = α_j + α_t + Σ_{l=1}^{L} β_l Y_{j,t-l}
                            + Σ_{l=1}^{L} γ_l X_{j,t-l}  +  ε_{j,t+h}

where j indexes industries, t indexes quarters, and the fixed effects absorb
common time variation (aggregate cycle) and industry-level means.

β_l captures own-series predictability; γ_l captures cross-series Granger
predictability.  At h=0, Y_{j,t} is regressed on lags only — so the
contemporaneous relationship does not enter.

We report:
  - γ_l coefficients (cross-series lags) at each horizon
  - Joint F-test (Wald) on {γ_1, ..., γ_L}: can we reject that cross-series
    lags are jointly zero?
  - Cumulative IRF of Y to a 1-SD shock in X_{t-1}, tracing how a past
    X shock propagates into Y over the next H quarters

Run
---
    python part2c_granger_lp.py

Prerequisites
-------------
    python part2_shock_rates.py
    python part2_shock_rates_s.py
"""

